1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:04,015
Hello and welcome to The Owl's Lessons!

2
00:00:03,715 --> 00:00:09,238
Last time, you discovered jacquet and trictrac, the French cousins of backgammon.

3
00:00:10,876 --> 00:00:16,852
Today, we're going back in time. Backgammon is one of the oldest board games still played.

4
00:00:16,552 --> 00:00:20,384
Ready for a journey of several thousand years? Let's go!

5
00:00:24,138 --> 00:00:32,267
About four thousand five hundred years ago, in Mesopotamia, in present-day Iraq, people were already playing the Royal Game of Ur.

6
00:00:32,417 --> 00:00:36,668
A board from this game was found in the royal tombs of the city of Ur.

7
00:00:36,368 --> 00:00:39,264
The pieces move forward according to the dice.

8
00:00:39,696 --> 00:00:41,877
In Egypt, people played senet.

9
00:00:41,577 --> 00:00:48,521
These are not yet backgammon, but the idea is already there: racing your pieces to the rhythm of the dice.

10
00:00:51,210 --> 00:00:52,571
Next stop, Rome.

11
00:00:52,271 --> 00:00:58,940
The Romans played the game of twelve lines, then tabula, the most direct ancestor of backgammon.

12
00:01:00,918 --> 00:01:08,117
In tabula, you already find twenty-four points, fifteen checkers per player, and the idea of hitting lone checkers.

13
00:01:08,631 --> 00:01:13,354
A Greek text even tells of a game lost by Emperor Zeno, in the fifth century.

14
00:01:13,054 --> 00:01:19,386
His position looks very much like modern backgammon… except that it was played with three dice back then!

15
00:01:22,627 --> 00:01:25,721
Meanwhile, in Persia, people play nard.

16
00:01:26,054 --> 00:01:32,801
A famous legend, told in the poet Ferdowsi's Book of Kings, credits its invention to the sage Bozorgmehr.

17
00:01:33,927 --> 00:01:37,464
An Indian king had sent the game of chess as a riddle.

18
00:01:37,164 --> 00:01:43,817
Bozorgmehr is said to have solved it, then answered by inventing nard, a game where the dice set the pace.

19
00:01:45,745 --> 00:01:53,110
It's only a legend. But nard, or nardy, is still widely played today, in Iran, the Caucasus and Russia.

20
00:01:55,536 --> 00:02:02,065
In the Middle Ages, in Europe, tables games are everywhere: in castles and in taverns alike.

21
00:02:03,742 --> 00:02:09,014
The Church bans them several times, without much success: everyone keeps playing!

22
00:02:09,870 --> 00:02:17,917
In twelve eighty-three, King Alfonso the Tenth of Castile even has a great book of games written, describing many variants of tables.

23
00:02:21,645 --> 00:02:25,548
The word "backgammon" appears in England in the seventeenth century.

24
00:02:26,087 --> 00:02:32,120
In seventeen forty-three, Edmond Hoyle publishes a short treatise that sets the rules of the game.

25
00:02:31,820 --> 00:02:33,583
It is still quoted today.

26
00:02:34,340 --> 00:02:40,372
In France, at the same time, trictrac shines at the king's court, followed by jacquet in families.

27
00:02:40,072 --> 00:02:42,712
You've known them since the last lesson!

28
00:02:46,130 --> 00:02:50,153
In the twentieth century, a small invention changes everything.

29
00:02:49,853 --> 00:02:54,703
In the nineteen-twenties, players in New York clubs invent the doubling cube.

30
00:02:56,185 --> 00:02:59,415
This die lets you double the value of the game.

31
00:02:59,115 --> 00:03:04,776
The game becomes far more strategic: you need to know when to offer, accept or refuse.

32
00:03:05,590 --> 00:03:10,288
In the sixties and seventies, backgammon is all the rage around the world.

33
00:03:09,988 --> 00:03:15,102
Prince Alexis Obolensky then organises the first major international tournaments.

34
00:03:16,558 --> 00:03:21,188
And since the late seventies, the world championship has been played in Monte Carlo.

35
00:03:23,474 --> 00:03:29,516
In the early nineties comes a revolution: TD-Gammon, a program created at IBM.

36
00:03:30,538 --> 00:03:35,291
It learns to play on its own, by playing millions of games against itself.

37
00:03:34,991 --> 00:03:37,999
And it reaches the level of the best players!

38
00:03:38,281 --> 00:03:45,331
It even overturns ideas accepted for decades: some openings played by the champions were not the best.

39
00:03:45,031 --> 00:03:47,714
The humans had to go back to school!

40
00:03:49,037 --> 00:03:54,575
Then come even stronger programs, like GNU Backgammon, which is free and open source.

41
00:03:54,275 --> 00:03:58,643
Today, players analyse their games with these programs to improve.

42
00:04:01,611 --> 00:04:07,690
And today? Backgammon is played around the clock, online, against opponents from all over the world.

43
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:13,208
On TickGammon, you can play against other players, or against a bot based on GNU Backgammon.

44
00:04:13,724 --> 00:04:17,746
You can also analyse your moves, take lessons and join tournaments.

45
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:22,965
A modern way to carry on a game that is thousands of years old…

46
00:04:22,665 --> 00:04:26,150
with the same fifteen checkers and the same two dice!

47
00:04:28,050 --> 00:04:39,204
And there you go, you now know the great history of backgammon: from the Royal Game of Ur to Roman tabula, from Persian nard to the New York doubling cube, all the way to computers.

48
00:04:39,354 --> 00:04:43,973
Every game you play is a game that generations of players loved before you.

49
00:04:45,267 --> 00:04:53,712
To keep the adventure going, play backgammon for free against other players or against the computer, in your browser, on Android or on Windows.

50
00:04:53,412 --> 00:04:59,294
Next time, the last lesson of the season: the player's vocabulary. Subscribe so you don't miss it!

51
00:04:58,994 --> 00:05:01,003
See you soon around the board!

